  "id": "COMPOUND:32586 - PROTEIN:6231",
  "verbalization": "The visible evidence suggests that Hirsutatin B may interact with Histone-lysine N-methyltransferase, H3 lysine-9 specific 5 through a series of target relationships. Specifically, Hirsutatin B is directly linked to the EZH2/SUZ12/EED complex, which is a known target of multiple compounds, including (+)-Phylliroside, quillaic acid, and Dimethyl 3-hydroxyphthalate. These compounds are all capable of modulating the activity of the EZH2/SUZ12/EED complex, which in turn influences the methylation of histone H3 lysine 9. This indicates that Hirsutatin B may exert its effects through a common pathway involving the EZH2/SUZ12/EED complex, ultimately affecting histone methylation. The consistent scores across the three paths suggest a robust and reliable relationship, though the exact mechanism by which Hirsutatin B influences the EZH2/SUZ12/EED complex remains to be fully elucidated.",
